- [x] I have searched the [Issue Tracker](https://github.com/tile-ai/tilelang/issues) that this hasn't already been reported. (comment there if it has.)

### What version of TileLang are you using?

0.1.15. Also seen on 0.1.7.post2. `src/cuda/transform/producer_consumer_ws.cc` is unchanged from v0.1.15 to `main` (c819a83f).

### System information

- NVIDIA H200 (sm_90a), driver 580.126.20, CUDA 13.0
- Python 3.13, PyTorch 2.14.1+cu130
- Kernels are called from JAX through `jax_tvm_ffi`, in multi-node training (8 GPUs per node, NCCL over InfiniBand)

### Problem description

Rarely, one block of a warp-specialized, TMA-pipelined attention kernel never finishes. That GPU sits at 1 of
132 SMs busy and every other rank waits in NCCL. There is no error and no traceback; the job just stops making
progress. In 64-GPU training it happened at steps 400, 1,800, 4,200, 8,300 and 9,600 across five runs.

For a TMA-only producer, `ProducerConsumerWSRewriter` runs the producer loop in all 128 producer threads, but only
the elected thread in warp 0 issues loads. Warps 1-3 execute only the loop's `mbarrier.wait`s, and nothing keeps
them in step with warp 0. If one of them falls two phases behind, the parity it waits for has already completed
and been reused. By then the consumers have exited, so that phase never completes again and the warp spins forever.

Generated producer loop for `examples/flash_attention/example_mha_fwd_bshd.py` (8, 32, 4096, 128), stock 0.1.15:

```cuda
if (((int)threadIdx.x) < 128) {
    tl::warpgroup_reg_dealloc<24>();
    for (int k = 0; k < 32; ++k) {
      mbarrier[2].wait(((k & 1) ^ 1));          // all 4 producer warps wait here
      if (tl::tl_shuffle_elect<128>()) {        // but only one thread issues
        mbarrier[0].arrive_and_expect_tx(32768);
        for (int i_1 = 0; i_1 < 2; ++i_1) {
          tl::tma_load(K_desc, mbarrier[0], ...);
        }
      }
      mbarrier[3].wait(((k & 1) ^ 1));          // warps 1-3 only ever execute these waits
      if (tl::tl_shuffle_elect<128>()) {
        mbarrier[1].arrive_and_expect_tx(32768);
        for (int i_2 = 0; i_2 < 2; ++i_2) {
          tl::tma_load(V_desc, mbarrier[1], ...);
        }
      }
    }
  } else {
    tl::warpgroup_reg_alloc<240>();
    ...
```

**Evidence (per-warp capture, 0.1.7.post2).** We wrapped every generated `mbarrier.wait` with a timeout reporter
that records the last code point of each warp. In 4 of 4 captured hangs, on a single-node config that hung within
1-4,200 steps, exactly one warp on the GPU had not exited. It was a non-issuing producer warp (warp 1, 2 or 3)
in the loop's `mbarrier[3].wait(parity 0)` on the last iteration. That barrier has arrival count 128 and is
arrived on only by the consumers. Its state token read phase 0, 128/128 arrivals pending, no tx pending: a fresh
phase that nobody will arrive on.

**Evidence (0.1.15).** On 0.1.15 the producer is generated the same way (above). In our 64-GPU hangs, kernel
progress markers showed the stuck GPU inside a warp-specialized flex-attention forward kernel with this producer
structure. We did not capture per-warp state on 0.1.15.

**Workaround.** Making producer warps 1-3 exit right after `setmaxnreg` (by post-processing the generated CUDA)
removed the hang:
- 1 node (0.1.7.post2): two runs of 15,800 steps each, no hang, on the config that hung within 1-4,200 steps.
- 64 GPUs, 45B model (0.1.15)

