### What version of TileLang are you using?

A source build at main `47976f2` (version string `0.1.15+cu129.git47976f21`), and the 0.1.15 release. Both generate the pattern below; the generated code shown is from main.

### System information

- Main: source build at `47976f2`, CUDA 12.9.86, GCC 11.5, Python 3.13, torch 2.14.1; code generation for `sm_90a`.
- Runtime tests below: TileLang 0.1.15 from PyPI, CUDA 13.2, on an H200.

### Problem description

When a shared→global `T.copy` is lowered to a TMA (bulk) store, TileLang emits the async-proxy fence only in the thread that issues the store, after the barrier that joins the threads that wrote the shared tile. The other writing threads execute no `fence.proxy.async` after their writes.

For example, here is the epilogue of `examples/flash_attention/example_mha_fwd_bhsd.py`:
- source: `T.copy(acc_o, O_shared)` then `T.copy(O_shared, Output[...])`, lines 99–100;
- call: `flashattn(1, 1, 256, 256, 64, False, block_M=64, block_N=64, num_stages=1, threads=128)`;
- code: as `kernel.get_kernel_source()` prints it on main `47976f2` (lines 228–239; indices abbreviated, comments ours).

```cuda
tl::__sync_thread_partial(3, 128);
#pragma unroll
for (int i_8 = 0; i_8 < 4; ++i_8) {
  // all 128 consumer threads write their fragments of O_shared (generic proxy)
  tl::ptx_stmatrix_m8n8_x4(&O_shared[...], ...);
}
tl::__sync_thread_partial(3, 128);
if (tl::tl_shuffle_elect<128>()) {
  tl::fence_proxy_async();                         // only the elected thread fences
  tl::tma_store(&Output[...], &O_shared[0], 8192); // async-proxy read of the whole tile
  tl::tma_store_arrive();
  tl::tma_store_wait<0, true>();
}
```

In the PTX, `fence.proxy.async.shared::cta` and the `cp.async.bulk.global.shared::cta` store both come after the `bar.sync`, inside the elected thread's branch. The 0.1.15 release generates identical CUDA for this call.

NVIDIA's documented pattern is different. Every thread that wrote the shared buffer executes the proxy fence, then the barrier, then one thread issues the TMA store.
- The CUDA Programming Guide's TMA example has each thread call `ptx::fence_proxy_async(ptx::space_shared)` (`fence.proxy.async.shared::cta`) before `__syncthreads()`, with the comment "After syncthreads, writes by all threads are visible to TMA engine".
- CUTLASS's SM90 TMA epilogue (`sm90_epilogue_tma_warpspecialized.hpp`) fences the same way, though one warp rather than one thread then issues the store:

```cpp
cutlass::arch::fence_view_async_shared(); // ensure smem writes are visible to TMA
synchronize(); // ensure all threads have issued their async fence
```

FlashAttention-3's epilogue and Triton's TMA stores also fence in every writer before the barrier.

The PTX ISA does not settle whether TileLang's placement is enough. Section 9.7.15.4 (membar/fence) says bi-directional proxy fences "take effect within a single thread". Their cross-proxy effect "composes with other forms of synchronization according to the rules of the Memory Consistency Model". The memory-model chapter (§8.9.5) states a causality-path rule for proxy fences only for virtual aliases, not for the async proxy. So the text does not say whether one thread's fence covers generic writes that other threads made before a barrie

