### Question

Does the pipelined lowering of the [shipped `gemm_tcgen5mma.py` example](https://github.com/tile-ai/tilelang/blob/a35f8ddf45eba16c21211ec8822d56ce5363036f/examples/gemm_tcgen05/gemm_tcgen5mma.py) need to keep a shared-memory stage live until its `mbarrier_wait_parity` completes? In the generated code, the next copy into that stage is issued before the wait.

I reproduced the compiler output with TileLang **v0.1.15** (tag `a35f8ddf45eba16c21211ec8822d56ce5363036f`), `sm_100a`, `M=N=4096`, `K=8192`, 128×128×128 blocks, `num_stages=2`, and 256 threads. The example's own `TL_DISABLE_TMA_LOWER=True` and `TL_DISABLE_WARP_SPECIALIZED=True` settings were retained. The source loop orders `T.copy(A/B)`, `T.tcgen05_gemm(..., mbar=mbar)`, then `T.mbarrier_wait_parity(mbar, k % 2)`.

The generated CUDA loop has this order (line numbers from the generated file; expressions shortened):

```cpp
// 81, 83: async MMA reads shared stage k & 1 and commits to mbar[0]
tl::tcgen05mma_ss(..., desc_a_for_stage(k & 1), ...);
tl::tcgen05_mma_arrive(&mbar[0]);
// 86-88: thread-sync fences and __syncthreads()
tl::tcgen05_before_thread_sync();
__syncthreads();
tl::tcgen05_after_thread_sync();
// 91, 95: cp.async writes tile k+2 into that same stage
tl::cp_async_gs<16>(&A_shared[(k & 1) * 16384 + ...], ...);
tl::cp_async_gs<16>(&B_shared[(k & 1) * 16384 + ...], ...);
// 98: the MMA completion wait comes after those copies are issued
mbar[0].wait(k & 1);
```

The fence helpers emit `tcgen05.fence::before_thread_sync` / `after_thread_sync`; they do not wait on the MMA completion barrier. My reading of the [PTX ISA synchronization rules](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#tcgen-async-sync-operations-commit) is that the thread-sync fence and `bar.sync` alone do not establish completion of the asynchronous shared-memory reads. Is there another guarantee that makes the early `cp.async` refill safe, or should `PipelinePlanning` keep the shared operands live through the paired wait?

### Control and evidence boundary

Changing only `num_stages` from 2 to 0 in the same source function keeps the source-order wait before the next iteration's copies. With one fixed input, our PTX verifier reports one **race candidate** for stage 2 (16-byte copy write against 32-byte asynchronous MMA read, overlap `[1024,1040)`; first witness, search incomplete) and `race_free` for the stage-0 control (search complete). These are static verifier results, not observed wrong values on hardware. The generated stage-2 CUDA SHA-256 is `9079a3114a8b7e95afdbbbf386aaf4aa25663b57f53fddc6cad42d8c3582a63f`; its raw PTX SHA-256 is `f3458618de9fcd1d034318ab3510f2959d8894a4519384f3c688f29baf35ae49`.

